WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.630
OK.

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:06.800
If you are protesting now or if you plan to
protest someday, you should really know your

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:07.800
rights.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.960
What is the law and what is beyond the law
so that you can be prepared to advocate for

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.289
yourself and others if you see protesters
rights being violated and so that you can

00:00:17.289 --> 00:00:18.289
remain safe.

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:23.369
Now, I know I don't even have to say this,
but of course, if you break the law, including

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.740
by damaging property or attacking people,
you are at risk of getting arrested and of

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.310
getting the crap kicked out of you.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.600
By the way, the way things seem to be going
with police and nothing in this video is meant

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.070
to help people break the law.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:39.260
The point is to understand the law and understand
what you're legally allowed to do.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:45.329
So first of all, the core of this is that
the First Amendment of the to the Constitution

00:00:45.329 --> 00:00:48.950
of the United States grants you the right
to assemble.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:54.050
To use your voice to protest, using your voice
includes things like holding signs as well.

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:59.739
It is legal for law enforcement to ban protesters
from certain physical spaces.

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:03.930
For example, if law enforcement says you can't
walk on the highway to protest.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:04.989
And there's already.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.850
Most states have rules against walking on
the highway anyway.

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:08.850
And you do it.

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.799
You can get arrested for that.

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:15.340
If there is a curfew and you ignore it to
protest, you can get arrested.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:20.979
Where things get tricky is in the gray area
when you have 99 percent of a protest being

00:01:20.979 --> 00:01:24.499
peaceful and one percent starts breaking the
law.

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:28.469
The police do have the right to clear the
entire protest.

00:01:28.469 --> 00:01:32.169
And you can get arrested even if you weren't
doing anything wrong.

00:01:32.169 --> 00:01:35.109
If after police have said it's time to go.

00:01:35.109 --> 00:01:36.920
You stick around.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:44.279
Now, police are not supposed to immediately
start arresting and tear gassing and hitting

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:45.529
people with batons.

00:01:45.529 --> 00:01:50.069
If they say protest has to end, they wait
five seconds and they start beating the crap

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:51.090
out of people.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.069
They're supposed to provide a reasonable amount
of time for people to get out of the area.

00:01:55.069 --> 00:01:59.899
And if you are, what, clearly walking away,
you also should not be bothered or or attacked

00:01:59.899 --> 00:02:00.899
by police.

00:02:00.899 --> 00:02:06.539
Now, this is, as you can imagine, dangerous
because these rules can be misused.

00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:14.040
Police can incite a couple of people to break
the law, to then justify clearing an entire

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.040
protest.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.040
That's a way in which police can misuse these
rules.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:24.690
But understand that when part of a protest
has gone bad, legally, I'm not saying morally,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:27.810
legally, police can clear the entire area.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:33.359
Now, in terms of where you can protest, I
mentioned highways could be off limits.

00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:36.719
That would not be considered a limitation
of your First Amendment rights if police say

00:02:36.719 --> 00:02:39.540
you can't walk on the highway and do your
protests there.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.920
There are states that require permits.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.880
If you're going to be in the street or sometimes
on the sidewalk, if you're not going to be

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.239
moving and you're going to be blocking traffic
now, pedestrian traffic generally, you do

00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:57.670
not need permits to protest while walking
along sidewalks or even in streets.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.549
Sometimes if you are not obstructing traffic
depends on the state.

00:03:01.549 --> 00:03:05.359
It's very hard to protest on a road without
obstructing traffic.

00:03:05.359 --> 00:03:09.499
So the road protests almost always do need
a permit.

00:03:09.499 --> 00:03:14.030
Generally speaking, public spaces can be protest
locations that include sidewalks and parks

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.069
and the like doesn't mean you can do it without
a permit in some states.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:24.170
You must know the laws of your state any time
that you block the passage of people or cars

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:28.810
and this includes on sidewalks, you can be
arrested if you refuse to move.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.060
Understand that.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.459
And again, this can be misused by police.

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:38.889
Police will sometimes say you're blocking
pedestrian traffic and then say, and because

00:03:38.889 --> 00:03:41.079
of that, it's time to wrap it up.

00:03:41.079 --> 00:03:46.050
And then you stay and you get arrested, even
though traffic may not actually have been

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:47.050
blocked.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:53.860
Police are supposed to provide a reasonable
opportunity to comply, and that includes sufficient

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.780
time and a clear, unobstructed exit path.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:03.129
We had reports from yesterday that protesters
were sort of corralled onto one of the bridges

00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:04.529
in between Manhattan and Brooklyn.

00:04:04.529 --> 00:04:06.250
I believe the Manhattan Bridge.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:11.200
And that would be in violation of if you say
it's time to go, you have to provide an unobstructed

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.480
exit path and time to exit.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:19.180
Now, this means that they can't say the party's
over and within 15 seconds start arresting

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:20.180
people.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:26.229
But it is sometimes misused by police to claim
we issued a dispersal order and they didn't

00:04:26.229 --> 00:04:27.229
move.

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:30.060
A quick note about government buildings.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.990
Government buildings can be exceptions there.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.590
There's sort of public buildings in a sense.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:38.300
That doesn't mean you can necessarily protest
inside, and that varies by state.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.300
Very important.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.860
If you want to show up and record with your
phone or with a camera or whatever, you can

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:49.330
record what is going on in public and you
can record police no matter what they tell

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:50.330
you.

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:57.750
Again, the trick to watch out for is that
if by recording you are impeding police where

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.860
you've decided to stand, if you're getting
in the way of what police are doing, they

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.139
can argue you are interfering with police,
you're obstructing law enforcement, as you

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.639
can imagine.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:09.340
That can also be misused.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:14.030
And you can find examples of police saying
that somebody who's really not obstructing

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:18.289
is obstructing by virtue of where they're
standing to record the advice we generally

00:05:18.289 --> 00:05:25.660
see is stand on the sidewalk to record if
the altercation is happening on the sidewalk.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:28.160
Make sure that you're not in the way of police.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:34.820
Now, in terms of recording, you are also protesting
in a public space, meaning you can be recorded.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.720
Your picture can be taken.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.440
Your face could be published in media without
your consent.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:45.259
Understand that when you go to a protest,
you're not starting an altercation because

00:05:45.259 --> 00:05:46.930
someone's filming you.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.190
But they do film you and then that is you.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:53.620
Somewhere that is legal because you are out
in public at a protest.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:06:00.449
Police do have pretty broad rights to maintain
law and order and uphold local laws.

00:06:00.449 --> 00:06:04.050
And this can sometimes be misapplied to filming.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:08.240
Be aware, police can't tell you that you have
to turn over your phone.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.550
They can't force you to show them stuff on
your phone.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.150
They need a warrant signed by a judge in order
to do that.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:20.840
Now, if police approach you because you are
recording, certainly remind the police that

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.379
recording in a public space is number one,
of course, legal.

00:06:24.379 --> 00:06:29.659
And number two does not count as reasonable
suspicion to believe you have committed a

00:06:29.659 --> 00:06:30.659
crime.

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:33.530
In other words, if police say they're there
approaching you because you're acting suspiciously

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:38.850
and all you're doing is filming, filming does
not count as reasonable suspicion.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:41.650
If you do break the law and get arrested.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:46.849
The normal search and seizure rules would
apply if police tell you you've got to delete

00:06:46.849 --> 00:06:48.680
a video from your phone.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:51.259
That is not a lawful order from police.

00:06:51.259 --> 00:06:52.790
And you don't have to do it.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:58.240
If police want to search your backpack or
your wallet or your fanny pack before or after

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.889
an arrest, you're under no obligation to allow
it.

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:06.210
And you can even say, I do not consent to
a search of my property.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.490
Police typically are allowed to pat you down.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:14.669
In some states, you do have to give police
your name, but you are not required to give

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:16.129
any other information.

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:22.509
Remember, counter protesters that might not
agree with you also have free speech rights.

00:07:22.509 --> 00:07:28.020
And police are supposed to treat both protesters
and counter protesters equally, according

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.100
to the ACLU.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.680
Police are permitted if they identify antagonistic
groups.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.650
Police are permitted to keep those groups
separated, but they should allow them to be

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:41.210
within sight and sound of each other.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.319
Otherwise, you're not really able to protest.

00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:45.199
You're not really able to counter protest.

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:50.150
If you can only do it out of earshot and visible
range of the people you are protesting.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.990
So then a few notes on police interactions.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.231
If police approach you and you want to talk
to you, you don't have to talk to them, but

00:07:56.231 --> 00:07:57.231
don't resist.

00:07:57.231 --> 00:08:01.830
Of course, it's better not to run away, but
simply ask, am I being detained or am I free

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:02.830
to go?

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:08.379
If they say you were not free to go, you are
able to inquire what is the crime I'm suspected

00:08:08.379 --> 00:08:09.969
of having committed.

00:08:09.969 --> 00:08:13.250
That has caused you to detain me if you do
get arrested.

00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:15.030
You also don't have to say anything.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.860
Again, in some states you do have to state
your name, but in many states you don't.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.949
The degree of cooperation you want to exhibit
is up to you.

00:08:22.949 --> 00:08:24.300
Depends on what you've been doing.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.759
Depends on your priorities at any time.

00:08:26.759 --> 00:08:31.340
You can ask for a lawyer and they are required
to provide you one.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.910
Now, can you be fired by your employer for
being at a protest even if you didn't do anything

00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:41.870
illegal, even if your participation is protected
by the Constitution?

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:44.630
Unfortunately, the Andrew answer is it depends.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:46.800
It depends on the state you live and work.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.770
It depends on what kind of job you have.

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:51.690
It depends on what kind of agreement you have
in place with your employer.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:55.570
If you have won, a lot of people don't really
have such agreements and that one is more

00:08:55.570 --> 00:09:00.310
complicated and you should really discuss
it with a lawyer or legal aid service.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:06.150
But there are many circumstances in which
your employer can dismiss you merely for having

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:10.430
participated in a constitutionally protected
protest.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.850
Very important to understand the law and your
rights when it comes to that.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:15.660
Let me know if I missed anything important.

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:20.200
Make sure you are following me on Twitter
at David Pakman.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:27.240
And make sure you're subscribed to the daily
